narrative:
## Counting Unique Visitors

Every time someone visits, we record their username (or IP address). But we only want to count each visitor once.

### Recording Visits

```xiom
let visitors: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();
visitors.insert("alice");
visitors.insert("bob");
visitors.insert("alice");
visitors.insert("charlie");
visitors.insert("bob");
```

After these inserts, the Set contains only `{"alice", "bob", "charlie"}` — three unique visitors despite five total visits.

### Counting Total vs Unique

We can track both numbers by maintaining a counter AND a Set:

```xiom
let total_visits: Int = 0;
let unique_visitors: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();

fn record_visit(name: Str) {
  unique_visitors.insert(name);
}
```

### Checking If Someone Already Visited

```xiom
if unique_visitors.contains(name) {
  io.println(name + " has visited before");
}
else {
  io.println(name + " is a first-time visitor!");
}
```

### Full Program

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let visitors: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();
  let visits: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();

  visits.push("alice");
  visits.push("bob");
  visits.push("alice");
  visits.push("charlie");
  visits.push("bob");

  for name in visits {
    visitors.insert(name);
  }

  io.println("Page views: " + string.int_to_str(visits.len()));
  io.println("Unique visitors: " + string.int_to_str(visitors.len()));

  if visitors.contains("alice") {
    io.println("Alice visited!");
  }

  return 0;
}
```

### The Pattern

- Use a **Vec** to record every event (order matters)
- Use a **Set** to count unique values
- Compute the difference to measure repeat visits

